From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "A.Cervantez" <155030226+Ywach404@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 14:03:07 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] runtime: let the head drive the view angles on visionOS

In the immersive space the eye images sat on planes on each eye's axis,
but the game's camera ignored the head, so the world followed the head
instead of the player looking around in it. The host now hands the
runtime its head yaw and pitch (rt_host_head_pose), and once a game
frame, at the first pump after each Present, the runtime writes the
game's view angles from them: yaw is the body's yaw plus the head's
turn, pitch is the head's own, clamped to the game's 89 degrees.

The angles are written absolutely into the engine's one view-angle
store (engine+0x314868, the QAngle VEngineClient006's GetViewAngles and
SetViewAngles copy, with no other state), rather than sent as mouse
counts, which would pass through sensitivity, m_yaw and a truncating
scale and drift. A turn the game makes itself between two writes (a
spawn facing, a teleport, a dialogue's look-at) is found against the
last write and kept in the body. Menus, dialogues and the character
sheet pause it; the body is kept, so the view on close is the same
world seen from where the head is now. The pose each frame stands for
goes to the backend before its Present, for the compositor's late
rotation; a pump between CreateMove and Present changes nothing, so the
pose is the one the frame was drawn with.

While the head drives the view the right stick turns the body instead
of looking: smooth at 150 degrees a second by default, or snap turns
of 45 degrees (controls.txt turn=snap, turn_speed, snap_angle). Create
plus Options recentres: the body takes the view's heading and the
head's facing now becomes straight ahead, with no turn of the view.
Options alone opens the menu on its press as before.

The composition is headlook.c, pure and covered by the input self
test: body plus head, the clamp, recentre, pause and resume, a game
turn kept, the write into a fake angle store, and 10,000 frames of a
wandering head with no drift.
